I don't want to bore the reader, but let's just say that I was
embarrassed when I found that there were more ghosts of me in the
Valley of the Dead than any other person --- this is on a public
server, mind. The castle had yielded me a spellbook of finger of death,
and this made things _much_ easier later on in the game, especially as
the only real dangers left were D, H, and &, and FoD could take care of
the first two easily. Gehennom was uneventful, except for perhaps
getting swallowed by Jubliex (a few MM's did him in) and Orcus' town.
There I'd tamed half the shades and skeletons, and let them fight the
other half. There was another magic marker, as well as a bag of
holding, but my bBoH from the wand of wishing was quite enough for my
purposes, so I later set the other BoH inside the castle.
    Orcus was quite a bother, as he kept teleporting away whenever I
shot him with magic missile. As pretty much everything left on the
level was a pet of mine, I was afraid to shoot one of them down and
thus had to maneuver around a bit, wasting valuable turns as Orcus
would disappear after I got a few shots in. I went a bit further than
this, looked around, and levelported back to Minetown for some candles.
Well, all Izsack had was candles - 6 stacks of them in fact. Of one
each, of course. Back at the VotD where I'd placed my stash, I wished
for a blessed fixed saddle and tamed a white dragon to ride, not having
a grey or silver one around. After spending a bit of time pressing 'z',
I wrested a final charge out of the spent wand of wishing and completed
my candle collection. (Thanks to all of the helpful posts about this!)
    I went down to search for the vibrating square, and a level or two
above it, I ran into a giant spider.
    "The giant spider bites! The giant spider bites! The giant spider's
bite was poisoned! The poison was deadly!"
So much for the dragon. I picked my saddle up and went down to find the
vibrating square, which happened to be almost right in the middle of
the level. I plopped 1 gold piece there (see how much of a miser I am!)
and went back up to pay some special people a visit. Back at the
castle, I tamed that silver dragon again, named it Inigo, and rode him
down to Vlad's tower.
    Isn't this a pleasant surprise, Mister Impaler? I've got a little
present for you..!
    "The finger of death hits Vlad the Impaler. The finger of death
bounces! Vlad the Impaler hits! Vlad the Impaler bites! Vlad the
Impaler hits!"
    Oh, I guess he's dead already. Well, in that case...
    "The magic missile hits Vlad the Imapler. The magic missile
bounces!
    "The magic missile hits Vlad the Imapler. The magic missile
bounces!
    "The magic missile hits Vlad the Imapler. The magic missile
bounces!
    "The magic missile hits Vlad the Imapler. The magic missile
bounces!
    "You kill Vlad the Impaler!"
Well, that wasn't too bad, was it? Thank you, speed boots! Back to the
Valley of the Dead, where I put on all seven candles, cancelling the
cursed ones from Minetown first, just to be sure, and then blessing the
whole affair. I just noticed that I'm writing in first person this time
around. I levelported to Vlad's Tower, and went downstairs for the
Wizard, which would be fine and dandy if I could find the bloody way
in! After digging around the wizard's tower on all the levels, I went
to the spoiler files to see what they could tell me.
     Ahh, a magic portal down below, eh? Well then, c'mon, Inigo, we're
going to meet an old friend of mine! The bottom levels were rather
dull, except for a bit at the zoo where I ran out of mana and had to
retreat for a few turns. The numerous storm giant tins bolstered my
already pretty high strength, and I went up to face the Wizard. "The
finger of death hits the Wizard of Yendor. You kill the Wizard of
Yendor!"
     Ugh. That was even more of a pushover than Vlad. Going out... "A
tower of flame erupts from the floor! The Book of the Dead -more-"
     Ok, so I'm seriously screwed. What can I do without a book of the
dead?
     "glows a strange shade of red, but is unharmed."
     Phew! Not that my armour did very well --- everything flammable
was at throughly burnt or would be soon. Going down, I rang the bell of
opening with its last charge (as I'd caught myself before using up the
whole thing at the castle!) and was sent into Moloch's sanctuary, where
the rest of my armour met its demise. I met up with myself a few more
times, and found the entrance to the temple.
     "The finger of death hits the high priestess of Moloch. You
murderer!"
     Oh dear... I'd read that Moloch would do nasty things to me when I
stepped inside, so I'd just killed the high priestess outright, not
knowing that she would be peaceful before I entered. Rats, how am I
going to get telepathy now, with 'h' genocided and at XP 23 or so?
Doesn't matter... I started the trek up to level one, getting pushed
back quite a few times but eventually making it, having to kill Orcus
(accidentally killing two pets and an aligned unicorn with the
missiles!) and Baezelbubb. (I'd bought off some other demon prince for
his asking price of 110 zorkmids, and I just happened to have just
enough!) Finally, I arrived at DL 1, after picking up an Angel on the
way. We walked into a shop at DL 2 and I took a wand of death out, as
well as a tin of shopkeeper meat. Even shopkeepers seemed puny against
such good pets, but that wasn't the case later on.
      Ok, so it's the plane of earth. I'd tamed the pair of elf-lords
waiting for me at the up-staircase, and they took care of the Elvenking
while I dispatched Rodney (for the 7th time) and the minotaur. I dug
around, taming earth elementals when I could and killing the rest. This
soon became extremely impractical after I'd depleted all of my wands of
digging, even after recharging one -- I'd swing at the rock, stop as
some earth elemental came up, keep swinging for five turns or so, get
frustrated and kill the thing, dig through the rock, have the ceiling
collapse around me, and having my rubble turn into a creature that I
had to kill.
       That wasn't the worst part: the invisible archons and ki-rin
kept on blinding me with their radiance and surrounding me in dragons,
giants, and purple worms, seeing how I'd already genocided V, h, T, L,
c, and P. After a few hours of unbelieveable agony and holding down the
enter key, I finally managed to squeeze through a gap in the monsters.
Phew! I picked off a few that managed to follow me, and decided to
snack on some stone giant meat.
      "You begin eating the stone giant. You choke over your food. You
die..."
      After all that, I choked on a stone giant?!?! I didn't even have
my amulet of magical breathing on?!?!
      Oh well, I guess it's a learning experience. So, getting to the
end game is possible with extremely good luck, then. Not bad, not bad,
and better luck next time... or...
      "Your amulet glows with a soft golden light, and disappears. You
survived that attempt on your life."
      Ah! My blessed amulet of lifesaving from the castle wand! Ha! And
a yellow dragon just dropped another one, too! (I'm not kidding!)
Great, great, let's put that on, and continue...
     Escaping back to the starting chamber, I finally dug a side tunnel
back to the left side, where I managed to find the portal. Ok, coming
through.
     "You arrive at the plane of air."
     I heard that this was really tough. Putting on my boots of
levitation in place of my speed boots, and a ring of conflict, I was
able to ease out of the ring of creatures that an invisible titan had
been summoning around me. I would have used FoD to clear myself out
quite easily, but by this time it had faded away quite completely, and
I'd neglected to bring the spellbook with me. Gah! I still had magic
missile, cone of cold, and charm monster, so I figured I could still
make it through.
     With permanent invisibility and a cloak of displacement that I'd
found in the graveyard outside of the Temple of Moloch, I was able to
block most of the melee, and I used my wands of magic missile when I
got swallowed by the air elementals. (I'd brought twenty-some wands to
the end-game, and this managed to prevent my unicorn horn and my bag of
holding from being hit by the malignant aura.) Overall it was far
easier than the plane of earth, and some nice creature revealed the
portal to me quite early on, so I just made a dash towards it and
arrived the plane of fire.
     The only non-throughly burnt piece of armour I had on were my
boots, and they managed to arrive at this state quite quickly. I put on
my ring of free action instead of my ring of conflict, and let the
creatures follow me around, as I'd managed to tame an archon of Ptah
that'd been flashing his radiance at me. It took quite a while to have
the portal revealed, and I'd quit slept over it. Finally I went through
to the plane of water, where I'd found a spellbook of detect monster
lying around, and got my twenty-sixth spell. The effect lasted for a
few hundred turns, and I found this to be even better than normal
telepathy. Following the bubble around was quite idyllic, and there
wasn't anything there to summon whores of nasties, so I just went along
until the portal was revealed, and jumped through.
     The astral plane was a bit harder. I re-cast detect monster, and
saw that there were a few angels of Thoth on the left, so I headed for
there. After leaving my angel several squares behind, I put on my ring
of conflict to try to clear things out a bit. Didn't work too well, and
that angel eventually caught up with me. I didn't dare to hit it and
invoke Thoth's wrath, so instead I let a samurai named Bob (or
something like that) finish him off for me. I had to pass through
Death's chamber, and one-hit him with a bolt of magic missile.
     Not bad... the angels were a bit annoying, and actually hit
through my -20 or so AC, so I quaffed a few of those potions of extra
healing. I finally made it to the left altar, and found it to be the
altar of Anhur. D'oh! Going back now... it wasn't quite as easy, as all
of the angels were close to me, and I'd put free action back on (paired
with regeneration, which was invaluable throughout) after something
paralyzed me with a thrown potion. As the major problem was squeezing
out of doorways, I could tame creatures now, and displace them for my
escape. I got back to Death, and this time he wasn't quite as easy,
draining my max hp down to 220 before I escaped from his clutches.
     Well, now for the middle chamber, which is probably neutral.
Guarding it was Pestilence, whose illness wasn't as much of a problem
as the 40-odd damage he did to me each time he hit. He caught up to me
as I was trying to squeeze into the altar room, and got me down to 17
hp, when I'd prayed to Thoth. The prayer worked, but he only removed
the illness, which I'd been doing quite easily with my blessed unicorn
horn throughout the game. So Pesty hit me again, and I died on the
Astral Plane with the amulet, so close to an ascension...
     But fortunately I was wearing one of the two amulets of lifesaving
that I'd picked up from dragons in the end game, and I put the last one
on. I made it inside the altar, and was about to offer it... to Anhur?
No, that wouldn't do at all... time to get out of this mess! I tamed a
red dragon at the door, and after a series of whacking angels with the
blessed rustproof +6 Frost Brand and displacing pets, I managed to slip
past Pestilence and make it out to the bottom of the Plane.
     I ran into a storm giant. I killed him. I pushed his boulder into
the 1-square wide corridor, and blocked all the nasties from the 2/3 of
the map I'd gone through, and congratulated me on my good work. Well,
there was only one altar left, and it had to be Thoth's. So here we go!
Oh, there's Famine. Time to take out my food rations! By this time I'd
used up most of my wands trying to get past the Riders and all of the
pesky angels, so I hurled the rest of my charges at Famine, and he
still didn't fall. Fortunately, an angel or something was shooting
beams at me from behind Famine, so he was being hit once from in front
and twice from behind (due to reflection), and eventually he was
vanquished.
     I rushed into his chamber, which was relatively empty, and had a
rather easier time making it to the altar of Thoth. At the entrance,
some desperate angel shot a final summon insects spell at me, which I
used charm monster on, and displaced them to reach the altar, and
immediately typed #offer 'a'.
     "You and your killer bee ascended to your prize..."



